                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="1" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="1" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we identified that your house has excess air leakage associated with the following locations:



** Around the front door, as the trim is missing



** The old exhaust fan in the bathroom



** Around the windows in the bedroom, and the window in the office



** The current chimney for the furnace, and the location where you had a second chimney. 



** The housewrap and insulation in your floor above the crawlspace is also falling down in places, and not sealed. While this is in the centre of the house, I strongly recommend that you replace the insulation and house wrap and air seal around all penetrations for piping and ductwork in the crawlspace. 



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $150 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 3.8 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The above foundation exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 with R20 batt insulation. I recommend you add a minimum R15 to the exterior walls, in conjunction with a future planned upgrade to your exterior siding. This will bring your walls up to approximately R30, which is in line with requirements for new construction.



The new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) that was launched on April 1, 2019 can help you cover the cost of the additional insulation. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added to the above foundation exterior walls ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).



Savings: By adding insulation to your above foundation exterior walls as detailed above, you could potentially save $570 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 13.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $5,000 in rebates from the DHERP.



Participation in this program also allows to you access additional funding for other upgrades, as well as freight for materials.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA, and my visit on 11 February 2021 satisfies the requirement for the pre-retrofit evaluation. 



This program has been extended until 31 March 2024, pending available funding. If you are considering taking advantage of this program, please contact me for more information. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</mainWalls>
                <windows>

All of the windows in this house are older double windows, all of which are double pane. Some are wood framed, some are vinyl. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $340 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 8.1 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your current electric hot water tank is quite small, and quite efficient compared to other models. However, because electricity in Tulita is generated using diesel, I recommend that you install an R5 insulation blanket on the heater, for further savings. </HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 83% efficient oil furnace. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a 95% AFUE condensing oil furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $1,280 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 27.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights ????????? Thermal Community



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal, and LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



I recommend that all lighting in your home be replaced with LED lighting, including both interior and exterior.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Fail



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 5.3 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. The current oil furnace and wood stove are both susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of this home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed and operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Hot Water Tank



Your current electric hot water tank is quite small, and quite efficient compared to other models. However, because electricity in Tulita is generated using diesel, I recommend that you install an R5 insulation blanket on the heater, for further savings. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



This home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

        - a range hood   

        - one bathroom fan

        - a clothes dryer



I recommend installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Tulita



The following Dec. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Tulita:



Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil - $1.51 per liter 

Wood - $735 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Alexandra Giroux, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: alexandra.giroux@aea.nt.ca

Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
